WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:08.460
The vast oceans of the popular video game
Subnautica teem with alien creatures — some

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:12.040
friendly — and some deadly.

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:17.670
While the game focuses on survival, the lifeforms
of Subnautica are amazing even when they’re

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:23.310
trying to eat you: possessing fascinating
biology, bizarre behavioral patterns, and

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:25.150
hidden mysteries.

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:31.770
So, for this entry into the archive, we’ll
embark on a documentary-style expedition,

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:37.339
touring ecosystems from the shallows to the
abyssal trenches; swimming in the shadows

00:00:37.339 --> 00:00:42.719
of giants, and evading predators of the deep.

00:00:42.719 --> 00:00:48.051
This series will reveal the biology behind
these puzzling lifeforms, and investigate

00:00:48.051 --> 00:00:50.719
how they differ from life in our own oceans.

00:00:50.719 --> 00:00:55.190
And although it’s been a while since this
Archivist has done any fieldwork, I’ll be

00:00:55.190 --> 00:01:02.329
diving into the perilous seas of planet 4546B
myself to capture footage of these magnificent

00:01:02.329 --> 00:01:03.329
lifeforms.

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:09.820
So, let’s take the plunge together, and
see what awaits us under the surface…

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:15.120
Beginning in the safety of shallow warm waters,
we find that stable conditions have led to

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:17.980
a region crowded with varied life.

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:23.040
Here, you can encounter all manner of undersea
organisms.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:28.431
One of the first lifeforms we can spot are
the Peepers… or rather, they spot us, with

00:01:28.431 --> 00:01:31.660
their enormous, dinner-plate like eyes.

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:36.990
These easily identifiable creatures have evolved
visual sensors capable of discerning colors

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:41.130
not just in clear waters but in a variety
of lighting conditions.

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:46.480
This, combined with their surprising speed,
means that despite being herbivores, Peepers

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:49.770
aren’t at the bottom of the food chain.

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:54.400
That distinction instead belongs to these
creatures, the rather unfortunate looking

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:55.550
Garryfish.

00:01:55.550 --> 00:02:01.370
Docile herbivores that are remarkably slow,
the Garryfish make easy prey for most anything

00:02:01.370 --> 00:02:03.480
else in these shallow waters.

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:07.960
All ecosystems, including the oceans of earth,
need something near the bottom of the food

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:09.240
chain, after all.

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:15.420
Other common herbivores on 4546B include the
Bladderfish, which swims by contracting and

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:21.550
expanding its translucent bladder, and the
unusual Boomerang, a horizontally symmetrical

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:27.390
oddity that possesses a mouth full of sharp
teeth to break down coral.

00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:32.920
On the subject of coral, the landscape of
the Safe Shallows is dominated by one particularly

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:35.030
unusual lifeform.

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:40.011
These gargantuan tubes are large enough to
swim through, and despite appearances, are

00:02:40.011 --> 00:02:43.870
very much alive.

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:49.780
Aptly called Giant Coral Tubes, they don’t
look much like the coral we know on earth.

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:54.450
These tubes have evolved to filter nutrients
that flow through their center, and judging

00:02:54.450 --> 00:03:00.800
by their size and abundance, it’s a strategy
that has been quite successful.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:06.590
Gliding near the tubes is the elegant Rabbit
Ray — the first of many ray species we may

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:07.590
encounter.

00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:13.020
The undulating motion that the Rabbit Ray
uses to swim through the clear seas is analogous

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:17.319
to the underwater movements of various types
of earth rays.

00:03:17.319 --> 00:03:21.660
But it’s not the movement of the Rabbit
Ray that gives it its curious name.

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:26.540
Instead, the Rabbit Ray’s most defining
features are its twin orange appendages that

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:29.569
help it sense vibrations.

00:03:29.569 --> 00:03:34.599
These ear-like structures resemble the ears
of rabbits, and so the aquatic Rabbit Ray

00:03:34.599 --> 00:03:40.310
shares a name with an otherwise dissimilar
terrestrial herbivore.

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:43.810
Beneath the shallows twist a network of underwater
caves.

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:48.880
While exploring one as I gathered this footage,
I heard a strange noise…

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:53.910
And a fish exploded in my face.

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:58.900
That short-lived creature was a Crashfish
— an evolutionary oddity that is difficult

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:04.630
to study due to its highly frustrating habit
of exploding when anything gets close.

00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:05.630
The Crashfish is defending the sulfur plant,
that provides the Crashfish its home.

00:04:05.630 --> 00:04:09.770
While an animal exploding to defend its territory
might seem too outlandish to evolve, there’s

00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:12.670
a model of such behavior on our own planet.

00:04:12.670 --> 00:04:18.320
Though uncommon, certain species of ants have
been observed erupting into goo to block tunnels

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:20.340
when their nest is under threat.

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:26.990
Truly, nature is highly unusual — no matter
the planet.

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:32.680
Elsewhere in the caves, the far less bothersome
Shuttlebugs drift near the stone walls.

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:37.810
Shuttlebugs are challenging creatures to make
sense of: with three legs and three mandibles

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:41.180
that have evolved into fin-like tentacles.

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:45.681
Despite looking slightly unsettling, analysis
suggests Shuttlebugs are too small to be a

00:04:45.681 --> 00:04:51.160
threat to most organisms, and have instead
adapted to feed on the waste products of the

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:53.630
ecosystem around them.

00:04:53.630 --> 00:05:01.620
Leaving the caves, you’ll see our first
large species is waiting for us: the Gasopods.

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:07.020
These rotund, leathery beasts resemble Earth
manatees, yet have a curious defense that

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:08.230
is all their own.

00:05:08.230 --> 00:05:13.470
Although not in any way hostile, when threatened,
the Gasopods will nonetheless release a deadly

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:18.830
cloud of underwater mist… which I can’t
recommend ingesting.

00:05:18.830 --> 00:05:23.340
Chemical analysis of the Gasopods tells us
the poisonous compound comes from the bulbous

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:26.370
appendage at the end of their tail.

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:31.480
Since Gasopods are social creatures, it’s
possible they even use these clouds to communicate

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:34.720
with one another.

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:39.220
But while the Gasopods are the largest creatures
in the Safe Shallows, they are barely the

00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:44.520
tip of the proverbial iceberg when it comes
to planet 4546B.

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:49.170
Venturing into the vast Kelp Forest, we’ll
find a radically different environment than

00:05:49.170 --> 00:05:50.760
the region we just left.

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:56.290
Here, the light is low thanks to the high
volume of creepvine plants — creating a

00:05:56.290 --> 00:06:01.750
hidden ecosystem, just like kelp-heavy regions
in Earth’s oceans.

00:06:01.750 --> 00:06:07.210
Here and there, clusters of Creepvine Seeds
cast shadows across the environment like natural

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:08.210
floodlights.

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:16.860
I find myself wondering just what might be
hidden among the swaying fronds…

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:22.889
That is a Stalker: the largest predator that
makes its home in the dense Creepvines.

00:06:22.889 --> 00:06:28.669
These fearsome customers possess a series
of dorsal ridges to help them make tight turns.

00:06:28.669 --> 00:06:34.270
Their long, thin snout is likely adapted for
ensnaring fish, as it greatly resembles the

00:06:34.270 --> 00:06:38.790
effective, slender snout of real-life gharials.

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:44.840
Yet at the edge of the Kelp Forest, one Stalker
is behaving… rather oddly.

00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:49.930
This Stalker is picking up various pieces
of metal scrap with its snout, only to drop

00:06:49.930 --> 00:06:51.970
them after a short period.

00:06:51.970 --> 00:06:57.100
This behavior seems to serve no practical
purpose, and indeed the stalkers seem to simply

00:06:57.100 --> 00:07:00.160
have a fondness for shiny objects.

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:05.220
Puzzling as this behavior might seem, the
barracuda — a predatory earth fish — is

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:09.680
also known to be attracted to shiny objects
because it’s evolved to spot the silvery

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:13.190
glint of the fish it prefers to prey on.

00:07:13.190 --> 00:07:17.699
And when one considers the common prey of
the Stalker, this attraction to shiny materials

00:07:17.699 --> 00:07:19.640
makes perfect sense.

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:24.639
In the murky depths of the Kelp Forest, schools
of fast-moving hoopfish light up the dark

00:07:24.639 --> 00:07:26.370
with their vibrant coloring.

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:31.600
A brief study of hoopfish anatomy reveals
they get their name from their antennae, which

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:36.090
extends from the top and bottom of their heads
and bends around their tails — forming a

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:37.110
hoop.

00:07:37.110 --> 00:07:43.580
Exactly why these stunning fish possess such
intense and varied colors, however, is a mystery.

00:07:43.580 --> 00:07:48.509
On the subject of mysteries, the last fish
of the Kelp Forest we’ll see is quite the

00:07:48.509 --> 00:07:49.509
enigma.

00:07:49.509 --> 00:07:54.130
Meet the Hoverfish: a peaceful herbivore that
appears to ‘hover’ on the pads at the

00:07:54.130 --> 00:07:55.900
ends of its six legs.

00:07:55.900 --> 00:08:00.539
Using these strange appendages, the Hoverfish
glides on the undersea currents in a manner

00:08:00.539 --> 00:08:03.510
quite dissimilar to any other fish on the
planet.

00:08:03.510 --> 00:08:08.690
It’s surprising to learn, therefore, that
the hoverfish may actually be a distant relative

00:08:08.690 --> 00:08:15.169
of the nondescript bladder-fish, with their
‘legs’ a highly derived form of the bladderfish’s

00:08:15.169 --> 00:08:16.850
primitive vacuole tubing.

00:08:16.850 --> 00:08:23.860
It’s the kind of creature that could only
exist in the Kelp Forest.

00:08:23.860 --> 00:08:29.160
Just beyond the waving Creepvines, the landscape
gives way to an alien Grassy Plateau.

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:34.849
Here, the sandy terrain is almost completely
carpeted with Blood Grass, as the seafloor

00:08:34.849 --> 00:08:37.580
is too deep for Creepvines to take hold.

00:08:37.580 --> 00:08:43.260
And in the open space, even larger lifeforms
are on the prowl…

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:47.120
At the edge of the Grassy Plateau, a strange
cloud of sand is gathering.

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:52.920
Be cautious—as we swim closer to the unexpected
turbulence, a creature with a huge, fanged

00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:54.990
mouth lashes out.

00:08:54.990 --> 00:09:01.029
This is a Sand Shark, a predator with a distinctive
hunting strategy.

00:09:01.029 --> 00:09:06.970
The Sand Shark burrows into the sand, then
erupts out when unsuspecting prey swims by:

00:09:06.970 --> 00:09:10.580
ending the chase with a snap from its massive
jaws.

00:09:10.580 --> 00:09:15.360
With segmented body armor and six rows of
fearsome teeth, it’s little wonder why these

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:18.990
predators are so effective.

00:09:18.990 --> 00:09:23.980
Alien as this behavior may seem, some sharks
on earth employ a similar strategy in swimming

00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:28.911
along the sandy floor near the shoreline in
search of food, although they don’t possess

00:09:28.911 --> 00:09:32.820
the extreme tunneling abilities of the Sand
Shark.

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:37.970
A common target of the Sand Shark’s brutal
ambushes swims overhead.

00:09:37.970 --> 00:09:42.730
The Spadefish doesn’t look like much from
most angles, but glimpse one from above and

00:09:42.730 --> 00:09:47.550
you’ll spot its defining feature: a single,
cyclopean eye.

00:09:47.550 --> 00:09:52.020
Closer analysis of the Spadefish suggests
it feeds on the sea floor, and its single

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:55.690
eye allows the spadefish to spot predators
above it.

00:09:55.690 --> 00:10:01.000
Its mottled green coloration suggests it normally
hides among sea grasses like many fish do

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:03.290
on earth.

00:10:03.290 --> 00:10:10.360
When a Spadefish wanders onto the Grassy Plateaus,
however, it is hopelessly exposed…

00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:15.700
On the far side of the Grassy Plateau, what
appear to be tiny floating islands hang miraculously

00:10:15.700 --> 00:10:17.270
in the water.

00:10:17.270 --> 00:10:22.580
Upon closer inspection, these chunks of rock
are all suspended by colonies of the same,

00:10:22.580 --> 00:10:25.620
curious lifeform: the Floaters.

00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:31.430
Transparent, gelatinous blobs, floaters are
at once oddly beautiful, and a little silly

00:10:31.430 --> 00:10:33.020
looking.

00:10:33.020 --> 00:10:37.570
Analysis of these creatures reveal they’re
actually multiple microorganisms living in

00:10:37.570 --> 00:10:42.330
symbiosis, which we can compare to some deep-sea
species of jellyfish.

00:10:42.330 --> 00:10:48.990
The floater’s ability to suspend such heavy
objects, however, remains something of a mystery.

00:10:48.990 --> 00:10:53.570
Swimming close by the floating islands is
a distant relative of the peeper called the

00:10:53.570 --> 00:10:54.860
Reginald.

00:10:54.860 --> 00:11:00.560
The entire back end of these oddly-named fellows
is composed of one large fin, and the fin’s

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:03.880
bright coloration appears to be part of a
mating ritual.

00:11:03.880 --> 00:11:09.550
Biologists believe the Reginald filters algae
and other plant material from the water via

00:11:09.550 --> 00:11:13.340
four gill-like, forward-facing orifices.

00:11:13.340 --> 00:11:20.840
While this is a noteworthy adaptation, something
looming behind the Reginald demands our attention.

00:11:20.840 --> 00:11:25.399
In the abyss beyond the edge of the plateau,
the largest organism we’ve yet to glimpse

00:11:25.399 --> 00:11:34.430
calls out through the deep.

00:11:34.430 --> 00:11:42.830
This is a Reefback: a leviathan-class organism
that exceeds lengths of 220 feet or 70 meters,

00:11:42.830 --> 00:11:46.240
and is, thankfully, quite friendly.

00:11:46.240 --> 00:11:50.980
Swimming closer to these gentle giants, we
can see they’re unlike any lifeform we’ve

00:11:50.980 --> 00:11:53.170
yet to glimpse.

00:11:53.170 --> 00:11:58.820
Possessing a thick exoskeleton, their shells
have become an oasis of life.

00:11:58.820 --> 00:12:03.860
Adult Reefbacks can support mini-ecosystems
on their back: including many types of coral

00:12:03.860 --> 00:12:05.300
and small fish.

00:12:05.300 --> 00:12:10.570
And nearby this adult is its baby: a much
smaller creature whose shell is not yet ready

00:12:10.570 --> 00:12:13.310
to support an ecosystem like its parent’s.

00:12:13.310 --> 00:12:19.340
I confess I spent some time just watching
these creatures drift in their pods, listening

00:12:19.340 --> 00:12:24.560
to them call out to each other with complex
sounds that suggest a surprising amount of

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:25.560
intelligence.

00:12:25.560 --> 00:12:35.839
With 
the sun now getting low, it’s time to venture

00:12:35.839 --> 00:12:41.880
deeper into the abyss, and enter the foreboding
final biome of the Dunes.

00:12:41.880 --> 00:12:48.920
In this fast, empty expanse, life often flocks
near hydrothermal vents.

00:12:48.920 --> 00:12:53.820
These great fissures on the sea floor release
a rich chemical soup into the water, much

00:12:53.820 --> 00:12:58.220
like those that support life in the depths
of Earth’s oceans.

00:12:58.220 --> 00:13:05.200
But it’s not life among the vents that concerns
me…

00:13:05.200 --> 00:13:08.040
A terrifying roar comes from above.

00:13:08.040 --> 00:13:13.090
A Reaper Leviathan strikes out in the abyss.

00:13:13.090 --> 00:13:18.250
This aggressive apex predator possesses four
mandibles at the front of its maw to drag

00:13:18.250 --> 00:13:20.060
in large prey.

00:13:20.060 --> 00:13:26.519
And at 180 feet, or 55 meters in length, a
hungry Reaper Leviathan can take down almost

00:13:26.519 --> 00:13:28.100
anything.

00:13:28.100 --> 00:13:32.500
Analysis of the organism’s biology suggests
the deep roar emitted by the Reaper is a form

00:13:32.500 --> 00:13:36.870
of sonar – meaning if you can hear it, the
reaper can see you.

00:13:36.870 --> 00:13:41.080
Thankfully, it seems this Reaper isn’t hungry.

00:13:41.080 --> 00:13:47.000
And as I watch the deadly leviathan vanish
into the darkness, I realize I’ve done enough

00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:50.670
field work for the day…

00:13:50.670 --> 00:13:54.209
The sun has now set on 4546B.

00:13:54.209 --> 00:13:59.160
Yet the wonders of this planet continue, as
in the low light, various regions of the sea

00:13:59.160 --> 00:14:03.810
are transformed by stunning displays of bioluminescence.

00:14:03.810 --> 00:14:09.050
Even the Reefback pod from earlier is now
giving off a striking glow.

00:14:09.050 --> 00:14:15.480
This eerie beauty suggests the many hidden
wonders we have yet to see beneath the surface.

00:14:15.480 --> 00:14:20.940
We’ll venture farther into the depths in
Part Two of the Biology of Subnautica, investigating

00:14:20.940 --> 00:14:26.380
the larger, more extreme, and often more dangerous
life of the deeper biomes.

00:14:26.380 --> 00:14:30.580
For now, though, our journey is at its end.

00:14:30.580 --> 00:14:32.720
As always, thanks for watching.

00:14:32.720 --> 00:14:37.260
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support to help makes these videos possible

00:14:37.260 --> 00:14:43.320
by liking, subscribing, and hitting the notification
icon to stay up to date on all things Curious.

00:14:43.320 --> 00:14:44.590
See you in the next video.

